1. git init = to consider a file/folder as a git repository

2. git add <name> = move to staging area ( git add * is used when there are multiple files )

3. git commit -m <message> = to commit file

4. git log = to view commit history and get IDs

5. git status = to check the current status  of file

6. git show <ID>:<name> = to view previous version

7. git checkout <ID>: * = to get back to the previous version ( allows to make changes )

8. git checkout <branch(master)> -- * = to come back to the current version that is using

9. git diff --cached = to view what changes is made

10. git restore . = to undo changes if accidently made

11. git restore --staged . =  if an accidental changes is made and moved to staging area so to get back
                             from staging area ( after this git restore . is used to undo changes )

12. git restore --worktree . = if a change is made knowingly and moved to staging and after it accidental
                               change is done and saved , so to rremove that error this is used

13. git reset --soft/hard HEAD^ = if an accidental error is done and did commit too then to move back to
                                  previous commit it is used

14. git log -p -2 = to view last 2 commits

15. git log --stat = to get summary of changes

16. git log --pretty=oneline = to view previous commit history and IDs in oneline

17. git log -S <func_name> = to view when a particular change is made ( func_name = h1 )

18. git log --grep="<search>" = to search for a particular word in changes

19. git log --since/until="2025-04-04" = to view the commits based on date

20. git log --author="<name>" = to view which user have done changes/commits

21. git push = to move the file to remote server

22. git pull = to take the file from remote to local

23. git clone <link> = to get access of the repository on linux terminal

24. git branch = to chekch which branch that we are currently in

25. git branch <branch_name> = to create new branch

26. git checkout <branch_name> = to move to the another branch

27. git merge <branch_name> = to merge the new branch with the main branch ( usually done when the
                              changes is done in other branch and it have to copied in main one also )

28. git push origin <branch> = used when only branch is showing up on github and the new branch also need
                               to be added to it ( branch = new_branch_name )

29. git forking === which allows to make changes to the code from another repo/account and merge to main
                    ( from another acc visit the acc which you want to fork , then click on fork you will
                      get the copy and then make the changes you want to make and click commit then send
                      pull request by clicking on contribution now the main repo acc will get notified in
                      the pull request tab there they can accept,review or reject the request then do
                      git pull to get the updated code in local )

30. sync fork == means after you forked from the main repo if there changes happend in the main one and
                  you want to copy those changes too then click on sync fork

31. .gitignore == when you dont wnat certain files to be tracked by git then you create a .gitignore file
                  and put those files inside .gitignore , this will not track the files ( this is done
                   because the folder is ofcourse need to be tracked but inside that folder there are
                  certain files that need not to be tracked ..)


32.git clean -n then git clean -f = deletes untracked files to get a clean repo

33. git tag -a <shortcut_name(v6)> <id> = to provide a shortcut like for a commit id ( better for
                                          recognizing a version )

34. git push origin --tags = to push tags to remote server i.e. github
